00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:08.840
A convex quadrilateral has diagonals that are perpendicular to each other.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:16.770
If three consecutive sides have lengths of three, two and one. What is the length of the fourth side?

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:19.410
I think I ask for the suggestion.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:27.400
Pause the video if you'd like to give this problem a try, and when you're ready, keep watching to learn how to solve this problem.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.260
Let's label some of the lengths in this diagram, we have w, x, y and z.

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.059
Consider this right triangle,

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:51.660
by the Gougu theorem, we have w squared plus x squared is equal to 3 squared.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:56.900
In this right triangle, we have x squared plus y squared is equal to 2 squared,

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:01.160
then in this right triangle, we have y squared plus z squared is equal to 1 squared.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.720
We want to calculate the hypotenuse of this right triangle.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:09.460
To do that, we'll subtract the second equation and then add the third equation.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:13.569
In doing so, we will cancel out the x squared and y squared terms.

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:20.109
We'll left with w squared plus z squared is equal to 3 squared minus 2 squared plus 1 squared.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:27.160
This means w squared plus z squared is equal to 6, the hypotenuse will be equal to the square root of this.

00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:32.919
Therefore, we have the square root of w squared plus z squared is equal to the square root of 6,

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.930
and that's the answer to this problem.

